<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= size => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(size); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? null : \old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i+1)) || (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null && this.get(i) == null);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; index<=i && i<\old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null) == (\old(this.get(i+1)) == null) && (this.get(i) != null => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1))));</code>

However, the above assert statement seems incorrect based on the natural language description. Let's refine it:

The comment states that for every valid index from the specified index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null if the old element at this position was null, or it equals the old element at this position. This means that the element at position `i` in the new list should be equal to the element at position `i-1` in the old list.

Here is the corrected assert statement:

<code>assert \forall int i; index<i && i<=\old(this.size()); this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i-1));</code>

This correctly captures the intended meaning of the comment.
